  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 206 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nThursday-Friday...northwest flow aloft to bring dry conditions and \nseasonable temperatures. Highs in the 80s and lows in the low 60s is \nforecast. Some additional wildfire smoke may also start to make its \nway closer to the Midwest, leading to more hazy skies and perhaps \nsome vibrant sunsets. \n\nFriday Night-Sunday...a weak shortwave trough to move through early \nin the period and low PoPs (<30%) have been maintained. However, \nforecast soundings show a lot of dry air and this may end up just \nbeing an increase in clouds. Otherwise, the active storm track to \nremain further north over the upper Midwest as pieces of energy \nrotate around the Hudson Bay low. Latest NBM trends have also \nlowered PoPs Saturday and early Sunday and I suspect this trend will \ncontinue. The exception to this is the 06z GFS where a fast moving \nshortwave may track over southern WI and northern IL and bring a \nchance of showers to northwest IL on Sunday. Otherwise, a warming \ntrend remains likely this weekend, as southerly low level flow \nadvects in warmer and moist air (850mb temps rising to 20-21C). NBM \ntrends in high temps Sunday have come down 2-3 degrees Sunday, \nlikely attributed to the warm bias from the GFS with readings \nnow solidly in the 80s. These temperatures combined with high \nprobabilities (>70%) for dewpoints in the 70s will result in \nheat indices in the mid 90s to low 100s Sunday. \n\nSunday night-Tuesday...weather pattern to become active once again, \nas several shortwaves move around the edge of the flattening western \nupper ridge/heat dome. The timing and evolution of these waves will \nbe key to storm chances and any severe/flash flood risk. With a warm \nand humid airmass in place, an MCS or two is not out of the question \nwhich would also throw a wrench into the temperature/humidity \nforecast.",
